@page "/logout"
@page "/signout"

@inject IBenUserState UserState
@inject IWebApiAuthService AuthService

@*
    The address people type.

    Signing out has always worked from the profile menu, and /logout answered "Page not found" —
    which reads as the site being broken at the moment somebody is trying to leave it, usually on
    a shared machine. It is the most guessable URL on the site and it was the only one nobody had
    written (2026-09-04 sweep).

    It asks rather than acting on arrival. A GET that ends a session can be fired by anything that
    can put a URL on a page — an image tag in a message is enough — and while a Blazor page needs
    its circuit before it does anything, "the framework happens to save us" is not the reason to
    rely on. One button is a small price for a door that cannot be pushed from outside.

    It waits for AuthReady before deciding which of the three things to say. Auth state is restored
    from storage after the first render, so a page that reads it immediately sees "signed out" for
    everybody — which this page did on its first run, telling a signed-in person they were already
    out while their own groups were listed in the sidebar beside it.
*@

<PageTitle>Sign out</PageTitle>

<div class="container py-5" style="max-width:480px;">
    <div class="card">
        <div class="card-body text-center">
            @if (!_ready)
            {
                <BenLoaderOverlay Visible="true" Text="Checking…" />
            }
            else if (_signedOut)
            {
                <h1 class="h4 mb-3">You're signed out</h1>
                <p class="text-secondary">Your session on this device has ended.</p>
                <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/login">Sign in again</a>
            }
            else if (UserState.IsAuthenticated)
            {
                <h1 class="h4 mb-3">Sign out?</h1>
                <p class="text-secondary">
                    This ends your session on this device. Anything you have saved stays where it is.
                </p>
                <div class="d-flex gap-2 justify-content-center">
                    <button type="button" class="btn btn-danger" @onclick="SignOut">Sign out</button>
                    @* An anchor, not a button: it only navigates, so it should work before the
                       circuit connects and be openable in a new tab. The repo guard says so. *@
                    <a class="btn btn-outline-secondary" href="/">Cancel</a>
                </div>
            }
            else
            {
                @* Said plainly rather than bounced to the sign-in form: somebody who typed this
                   address wanted to know they were out, and a login page is not an answer to
                   that question. *@
                <h1 class="h4 mb-3">You're already signed out</h1>
                <p class="text-secondary">There is no session on this device to end.</p>
                <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/login">Sign in</a>
            }
        </div>
    </div>
</div>

@code {
    private bool _ready;
    private bool _signedOut;

    protected override async Task OnAfterRenderAsync(bool firstRender)
    {
        if (!firstRender) return;
        await UserState.WaitUntilAuthReadyAsync(RendererInfo.IsInteractive);
        _ready = true;
        StateHasChanged();
    }

    private void SignOut()
    {
        AuthService.Logout();
        _signedOut = true;
    }
}
